{"agent":"bg21","data":{"by":"bg21","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"The AST check iterates only `ast.parse(harness.read_text(\"utf-8\")).body`, so it inspects only top-level import statements. If `ddflow/services/approvals/harness.py` has (or later gains) an `import urllib`, `import http`, or `import socket` nested inside a function, `if TYPE_CHECKING:`, `try:`, or other block, `roots` stays empty and the assertion passes. `importlinter` scans nested nodes, so `test_import_contracts` would still fail; this regression test therefore does not actually guard the contract against the drift it claims to prevent.","digest":"4ddd1dc6deb8f212","location":"tests/test_approvals_harness.py:test_harness_imports_no_http_roots_and_holds_no_http_home_entry","severity":"LOW","title":"tests/test_approvals_harness.py:test_harness_imports_no_http_roots_and_holds_no_http_home_entry"},{"chunk":1,"detail":"The import scan only walks `ast.parse(...).body`, so it never sees imports nested in functions, classes, or `if` blocks. Given `harness.py` whose only network import is `def load(): import urllib.request` (or the same under `if TYPE_CHECKING:`), `roots` stays empty, the intersection assert passes, and the baseline assert also passes if no ignore line was added. That is the bug class this regression claims to lock: the module still imports `urllib`, while `test_harness_imports_no_http_roots_and_holds_no_http_home_entry` stays green.","digest":"d981a40605750fa7","location":"tests/test_approvals_harness.py:test_harness_imports_no_http_roots_and_holds_no_http_home_entry","severity":"LOW","title":"tests/test_approvals_harness.py:test_harness_imports_no_http_roots_and_holds_no_http_home_entry"}],"chunks_total":1,"coverage":"1/1 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":2722,"diff_sha":"a4da24dd3c5148d1345cd7dd0a30023a0851c16af2c2f979cbcd4dd5aabc3e5b","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"in